WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:08.740
Imagine giving birth to
a 1,360-kilogram baby.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.800
That's practically the
weight of a small car,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.340
but it's the typical size
of a newborn blue whale,

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.700
the biggest baby in the animal kingdom.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.300
When they nurse, calves
put on 90 kilograms

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.730
every single day

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.500
thanks to their mom's extremely rich milk,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.050
which contains over 10 times
more fat than cow milk.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.050
So calves can grow to the size
of a Boeing 757 by adulthood.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.340
Sounds like a pretty cushy start to life,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.790
especially when compared
to other hefty newborns.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.770
On dry land, elephants win
the biggest-baby award,

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:51.750
though winning is a relative term.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.810
They give birth to a 105-kilogram calf,

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.300
which first had to gestate in mom's womb

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.430
for nearly two years,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.020
more than twice as long
as a human pregnancy!

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.480
Close behind elephants are giraffes.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.207
They drop a 75-kilogram calf, on average.

00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:13.400
Literally. Mom gives birth standing up,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.670
so the baby falls almost 2
meters to the hard ground.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.110
But it turns out that
tumble is pretty important.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.330
The impact breaks open the amniotic sac,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.023
allowing them to take
their first breath of air.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Next in line is the white rhino.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.090
Although not quite as long
a pregnancy as an elephant,

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.370
babies still gestate for 15 to 16 months.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.290
Once they're finally born,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.590
calves weigh 40 to 64 kilograms,

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.531
roughly the size of an
adult Saint Bernard.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.350
But here's the thing. If
you're about to give birth,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.650
you're not actually worried
about your kid's size.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.470
You're worried about their
size compared to you.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.700
And shingleback lizards
have it bad. Really bad.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.830
Unlike most reptiles, they
give birth to live young.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:10.210
Usually, twins that total a
third of their body weight.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:14.530
That's like a human giving
birth to a 7-year-old.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:18.740
Meanwhile, bats usually have
just one baby at a time.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.470
But that one pup is huge!

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.740
A fourth of their mother's body weight.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.020
That's equivalent to a human infant

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.740
the size of a 16-kilogram cocker spaniel.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.850
Meanwhile, horse foals usually are 1/10

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.410
of their mother's size.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.630
That's a bowling-ball-sized
baby, in human terms.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:38.910
Speaking of humans,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:43.140
our newborns weigh about
3.5 kilograms on average.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.410
Or 1/22 of mom's size.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.280
That might not sound like much,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.610
but we're actually pretty big.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.670
After all, tiger cubs weigh only 1/120

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.010
of their mother's size,

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.630
and a red kangaroo baby

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.480
is a minuscule 1/100,000

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.240
of mom's body weight!

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.420
That's right.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.010
A newborn joey can be as small
as a single grain of rice

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.230
and won't be ready to
venture from the pouch

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.150
for over a year.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.030
But the world's smallest babies

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.471
are a story for another time.

